.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt SETUID 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm setuid ,
.Nm setgid ,
.Nm setreuid ,
.Nm setregid ,
.Nm setresuid ,
.Nm setresgid ,
.Nm setgroups ,
.Nm setuid32 ,
.Nm setgid32 ,
.Nm setreuid32 ,
.Nm setregid32 ,
.Nm setresuid32 ,
.Nm setresgid32 ,
.Nm setgroups32
.Nd change process credentials
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Fd "#define _GNU_SOURCE"
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn setuid "uid_t uid"
.Ft int
.Fn setgid "gid_t gid"
.Ft int
.Fn setreuid "uid_t ruid" "uid_t euid"
.Ft int
.Fn setregid "gid_t rgid" "gid_t egid"
.Ft int
.Fn setresuid "uid_t ruid" "uid_t euid" "uid_t suid"
.Ft int
.Fn setresgid "gid_t rgid" "gid_t egid" "gid_t sgid"
.In grp.h
.Ft int
.Fn setgroups "size_t size" "const gid_t *list"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions change the calling process's real, effective, and saved
user and group IDs, or its supplementary group list.
Privilege for every function is determined by effective user ID 0,
including the group-ID functions.
.Pp
.Fn setuid
sets all three user IDs to
.Fa uid
for a privileged caller.
For an unprivileged caller, it changes only the effective user ID,
and the requested value must equal the current real or saved user ID.
.Fn setgid
applies the same rules to group IDs.
.Pp
.Fn setreuid
sets the real and effective user IDs.
An argument of
.Ql (uid_t)-1
leaves that field unchanged.
A privileged caller may choose any other value.
An unprivileged caller may set the real ID to the current real or
effective ID, and the effective ID to the current real, effective, or
saved ID.
The saved ID becomes the resulting effective ID if the real-ID argument
is not -1, or if the effective-ID argument is neither -1 nor the
pre-call real ID.
.Fn setregid
applies the same rules to group IDs, with
.Ql (gid_t)-1
for an unchanged field.
.Pp
.Fn setresuid
sets the real, effective, and saved user IDs independently.
An argument of
.Ql (uid_t)-1
leaves its field unchanged.
A privileged caller may choose any other value.
Each value requested by an unprivileged caller must equal one of the
three user IDs held before the call.
.Fn setresgid
applies the same rules to group IDs.
.Pp
The kernel does not treat -1 specially for the single-argument
.Fn setuid
and
.Fn setgid
calls.
The permission checks for a multi-ID call precede all changes.
.Pp
.Fn setgroups
replaces the supplementary group list with the
.Fa size
entries at
.Fa list .
Only a privileged caller may do so.
At most eight entries are accepted.
A zero size clears the list without reading
.Fa list .
The complete list is copied before the credentials change.
.Fn getgroups
with size zero reports the current count; see
.Xr getuid 2 .
.Pp
A call that changes the effective user or group ID also clears the
process's dumpable flag and its parent-death signal, as on Linux; see
.Xr prctl 2 .
.Pp
A change of real user ID makes the process count against the new user's
total for
.Dv RLIMIT_NPROC .
These setters do not enforce that limit.
.Xr fork 2
counts processes by their current real user IDs and compares the count
with the caller's soft limit.
Real user ID 0 is exempt.
.Ss System call numbers
Musl uses the i386 32-bit ID calls:
213 for
.Nm setuid32 ,
214 for
.Nm setgid32 ,
203 for
.Nm setreuid32 ,
204 for
.Nm setregid32 ,
208 for
.Nm setresuid32 ,
210 for
.Nm setresgid32 ,
and 206 for
.Nm setgroups32 .
It declares the functions without the
.Li 32
suffix and provides no separate prototypes for these kernel names.
The
.Fn setresuid
and
.Fn setresgid
declarations require
.Dv _GNU_SOURCE ;
.Fn setgroups
requires
.Dv _GNU_SOURCE
or
.Dv _BSD_SOURCE .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return zero on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/credprobe.c
sets supplementary groups 1000 and 2000 as root and reads them back.
It then sets only the effective user ID to 1000 with
.Fn setreuid ,
checks real ID 0 and effective and saved IDs 1000, and verifies that
.Fn setgroups
now fails with
.Er EPERM .
.Pp
.Pa tests/limitprobe.c
changes a child's real user ID to 1000 and verifies that it contributes
to that user's process count when a subsequent fork reaches
.Dv RLIMIT_NPROC .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
An entry of the supplementary group list cannot be read.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa size
exceeds eight.
This check precedes the privilege check.
.It Bq Er EPERM
An unprivileged caller requested an ID change outside the permitted
values, or called
.Fn setgroups .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr getuid 2 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2
